@@ -0,0 +1,44 @@
+---
+id: dl-003-safe-object-member-writes-via-object.defineproperty-in-applypatch
+type: decision-log
+title: "Safe object-member writes via Object.defineProperty in applyPatch"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+RFC 6902 §4.1 ("add") and §4.3 ("replace") require that, when the target is an object member, the
+operation create or overwrite that exact member. `addAt`/`replaceAt` implemented this with plain
+property assignment, `container[key] = value`. For almost every `key` this is a real, own, enumerable
+data property, matching the RFC. But JavaScript objects inherit an accessor property named
+`__proto__` from `Object.prototype`; assigning through that name with `=` does not create an own
+member, it calls the accessor's setter and replaces the object's internal prototype. A patch such as
+`{ op: "add", path: "/foo/__proto__", value: { "polluted": true } }` therefore applied without error,
+yet `foo` never gained a member named `"__proto__"` — `Object.keys`, `JSON.stringify`, and a
+follow-up `resolvePointer`/`test` on `/foo/__proto__` all disagreed with what the patch said it did,
+while reads of unrelated members on `foo` could start resolving through the new prototype.
+
+## Decision
+
+Introduced `setMember(container, key, value)`, which writes via `Object.defineProperty(container,
+key, { value, writable: true, enumerable: true, configurable: true })`, and used it for both `add`'s
+and `replace`'s object-member case in place of bracket assignment. `Object.defineProperty` always
+defines an own property with the literal name given, regardless of whether that name collides with an
+inherited accessor, so the result now matches RFC 6902 for every member name, including
+`"__proto__"`, `"constructor"`, and similarly-named members.
+
+No other write path needed the same treatment:
+- `remove`'s `delete container[key]` only ever acts on an own property (`removeAt` checks
+  `hasOwnProperty` first) and deleting an own data property is unaffected by this issue.
+- Every read path (`resolveToken`, used by `resolvePointer`, `test`, and the source lookup for
+  `copy`/`move`) already gates on `hasOwnProperty` before returning a value, so it was never at risk
+  of resolving through the inherited accessor.
+- Array containers validate the key as a numeric index or `"-"` before any write, so a key like
+  `"__proto__"` is rejected before reaching an assignment.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- `applyPatch` now treats every string as a legitimate object member name, including ones that
+  collide with inherited `Object.prototype` accessors; callers relying on the old (incorrect) silent
+  prototype mutation, if any existed, will now see the member actually added/replaced instead.
+- The fix is local to `addAt`/`replaceAt`; no public type or function signature changed.
