@@ -0,0 +1,49 @@
+---
+id: task-003-fix-applypatch-object-member-writes-to-avoid-prototype-interception
+type: task
+title: "Fix applyPatch object-member writes to avoid prototype interception"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Users of `applyPatch` report surprising results: some patches they consider invalid are accepted,
+some edge cases give results they did not expect, and a patch that fails does not always behave the
+way they read RFC 6902. Review how patches are checked and applied, and make the behaviour conform to
+RFC 6902 in every case, errors included.
+
+## What was done
+
+Re-read RFC 6902 (sections 3-5 and every Appendix A example) against the current implementation and
+ran both the official examples and a battery of ad hoc edge cases (array move direction, root
+operations, non-canonical indices, extra/ignored members, `test` type/order sensitivity, immutability)
+through `applyPatch`. All of those matched the RFC.
+
+One real conformance gap was found and fixed: `addAt` and `replaceAt` wrote object members with plain
+property assignment (`container[key] = value`). For the ordinary case this is a real, own, enumerable
+member as RFC 6902 §4.1/§4.3 require. But for `key === '__proto__'`, plain assignment does not create
+an own member at all — it invokes `Object.prototype`'s inherited `__proto__` accessor and reassigns
+the object's prototype instead. The patch was silently accepted (no throw) yet the resulting document
+did not actually gain the member the operation named: it was absent from `Object.keys`, from
+`JSON.stringify`, and from any later `resolvePointer`/`test` on that path — while unrelated property
+reads on the object could pick up values through the new prototype chain. This is exactly the
+"patch applies without error but the result is not what RFC 6902 describes" complaint.
+
+Fix: added a `setMember` helper that writes an object member with `Object.defineProperty(container,
+key, { value, writable: true, enumerable: true, configurable: true })`, which always creates a real
+own data property regardless of its name, and used it in both `addAt` and `replaceAt` in place of the
+bracket assignment. `removeAt`'s `delete container[key]` and all read paths (`resolveToken`) were
+already gated by `hasOwnProperty`, so they needed no change: they never resolved through the
+inherited accessor and were not affected.
+
+Added two regression tests to `test/apply-patch.test.ts`:
+- `add` creates a literal `"__proto__"` member instead of altering the object prototype.
+- `replace` overwrites a literal `"__proto__"` member without altering the object prototype.
+
+Confirmed red against the pre-fix `src/index.ts` (the `add` case failed; `replace` already happened
+to be safe once the member pre-existed as a real own property, since plain assignment to an existing
+own data property does not re-invoke the inherited accessor — only object creation through `add` was
+exploitable). Confirmed green after the fix, and confirmed the full suite (37 tests) and
+`tsc --noEmit` are clean.
+
+See `dl-003` for the design decision behind the `setMember` helper.
diff --git a/src/index.ts b/src/index.ts
